 ### [Monte Carlo Path Tracing for Volume Rendering](4980-monte-carlo-path-tracing-for-volume-rendering.md)
-Monte Carlo Path Tracing for Volume Rendering (MCSRender) is a stochastic method for simulating light transport within participating media by sampling scattering events along rays rather than discretizing the path into fixed steps. Unlike deterministic ray marching which integrates the volume rendering equation via quadrature, this method uses distance sampling to find interaction points and accumulates results over multiple frames using an exponential moving average to converge to the solution. It is distinguished by its ability to handle complex scattering geometries and anisotropic lighting via environment maps without requiring a precomputed light field or voxelized light transport data.
+Monte Carlo Path Tracing for Volume Rendering is a unified, brute-force, progressive stochastic rendering framework that computes photo-realistic direct volume rendering (DVR) without relying on pre-computed quantities or auxiliary data structures. It solves the problem of integrating complex lighting, shadows, and hybrid surface/volumetric scattering in participating media by treating the volume as a continuous medium where light transport is estimated via random sampling. Unlike slice-based or layer-based methods that treat isosurfaces distinctly, this approach renders the volume uniformly, supporting directional, point, and environment lights while maintaining a memory footprint insensitive to lighting or material complexity.
 
 ### [Ray Traversal Schemes](6890-ray-traversal-schemes.md)
-Ray traversal schemes define how scalar voxel values are aggregated along a ray to produce a final pixel intensity, ranging from simple statistical projections to physically-based compositing. They solve the problem of mapping 3D volumetric data to 2D image space by determining which voxel properties (intensity, opacity, depth) contribute to the final pixel color. Unlike [Ray Marching and Discretization](2788-ray-marching-and-discretization.md), which focuses on the spatial sampling of the ray, these schemes focus on the mathematical aggregation function applied to the sampled values. They differ from [Monte Carlo Path Tracing for Volume Rendering](4980-monte-carlo-path-tracing-for-volume-rendering.md) by typically using deterministic, sequential accumulation rather than stochastic sampling.
+Ray traversal schemes define the aggregation logic applied to the optical properties (color and opacity) sampled along a ray to produce the final pixel value. While the parent article [Ray Traversal and Integration Schemes](8341-ray-traversal-and-integration-schemes.md) establishes the standard front-to-back compositing loop, these schemes offer alternative accumulation strategies such as maximum intensity projection, averaging, or first-hit detection. They are distinguished from sibling schemes like [Ray Marching and Discretization](2788-ray-marching-and-discretization.md) by their focus on the *compositing rule* rather than the sampling geometry, and from [Image-order Rendering Loop](2938-image-order-rendering-loop.md) by their specific mathematical reduction of the 1D ray integral.
 
 ### [Multi-layer Volume Ray Casting](9441-multi-layer-volume-ray-casting.md)
-Multi-layer volume ray casting is a unified rendering framework that handles complex volume-of-interest (VOI) interactions, including clipping, CSG operations, embedded objects, and empty-space skipping, by encoding spatial overlaps into a pre-computed ray-layer buffer. Unlike standard single-layer ray casting which processes a continuous ray or polygon-assisted methods that store full 3D coordinates, this method decomposes the ray into discrete, non-overlapping segments defined by material IDs and Z-coordinates, enabling segment-by-segment rendering with material-specific transfer functions in a single GPU pass. It sets itself apart by shifting the computational complexity of spatial intersection and priority resolution to a preprocessing depth-peeling stage, thereby keeping the per-pixel ray-casting loop simple and GPU-efficient.
+Multi-layer volume ray casting is a unified rendering framework that handles volume-of-interests (VOIs)—including clipping geometry, embedded objects, editing masks, and non-empty-space boundaries—by encoding their spatial overlaps and priorities into a pre-generated ray-layer buffer. It differs from standard single-pass ray casters by casting rays segment-by-segment according to material IDs stored in the buffer, allowing distinct transfer functions and shading parameters per segment while maintaining GPU efficiency through a single rendering pass.
 
 ### [Ray Casting and Compositing Loop](9826-ray-casting-and-compositing-loop.md)
-This method defines the core computational loop for direct volume ray casting, responsible for sampling the volume, mapping data to optical properties, and accumulating color and opacity along a ray. It solves the problem of synthesizing a 2D image from a 3D scalar or vector field by evaluating the volume rendering equation. It is distinguished from [Ray Marching and Discretization](2788-ray-marching-and-discretization.md) by its explicit handling of the compositing accumulation and transfer function application within the integration step, and from [Monte Carlo Path Tracing for Volume Rendering](4980-monte-carlo-path-tracing-for-volume-rendering.md) by its deterministic, image-order approach.
+Ray Casting and Compositing Loop is the fundamental image-order rendering method for direct volume visualization, solving the problem of mapping 3D scalar field data to a 2D image by integrating optical properties along camera rays. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [IsoSurface Ray Marching](4324-front-to-back-volume-ray-casting-with-hsv-shading-and-sos-at.md) by avoiding explicit surface extraction, normal computation, or zero-crossing detection, instead relying on direct scalar-to-color mapping and front-to-back compositing. Unlike [Monte Carlo Path Tracing for Volume Rendering](4980-monte-carlo-path-tracing-for-volume-rendering.md), it uses deterministic integration rather than stochastic sampling, and unlike [Multi-layer Volume Ray Casting](9441-multi-layer-volume-ray-casting.md), it processes the volume as a single continuous entity without hierarchical layering.
 
-### Spherical Volume Rendering with Adaptive Ray Casting
-A method for expressing ocean scalar fields in a 3D digital Earth environment using a minimum bounding volume based on spherical mosaic as proxy geometry and adaptive sampling [S1, abstract only, unofficial]. It constructs a minimum bounding volume based on spherical mosaic as the proxy geometry, applies the depth texture of the seabed terrain to determine the position of sampling points in the spatial interpolation process, and proposes an adaptive sampling step algorithm based on heterogeneous depth distribution and data change rate to improve ray-casting efficiency [S1, abstract only, unofficial]. It also applies a nonlinear color-mapping enhancement scheme based on dataset skewness characteristics and designs a transparency transfer function to realize volume rendering and local feature structure extraction [S1, abstract only, unofficial].
