 ## Background
-The shear-warp algorithm is used in the field of computer vision as a fast method of rendering the rotation of 3D volumes. It is based on the principle that, for a given perspective, the projection of a 3D volume when rotated about the centre is equal to the projection of the same volume after it has been sheared and then warped, which in the case of the shear-warp algorithm is a scaling along the vertical axis (Y axis in Fig. 2), see Fig. 3(b) [S1, abstract only, unofficial]. In the context of deskewed lightsheet data, this means that wh… [S1, abstract only, unofficial].
+The concept of ray casting for volume rendering originated in the early 1990s as a direct solution to the visualization of 3D scalar fields without intermediate surface extraction. The shear-warp factorization, introduced by Levoy [S1, abstract only, unofficial], provided a fast method for rendering rotated volumes by decomposing the viewing transform into shear and warp operations, though modern implementations often rely on direct ray marching for flexibility. The method has become a standard technique in scientific visualization, medical imaging, and geosciences, evolving from CPU-based implementations to highly optimized GPU shaders and distributed parallel systems.
 
